/*  truncT -- A STATEMENT THAT FAILS TO MATCH ENDS THE FILE, AND NOW SAYS SO. F-79.

    `Start` is `StatemenT+`. A statement that fails to MATCH ends that repetition, so every
    byte after it is never parsed -- no error, no `stop:` line, exit 0, which is
    indistinguishable from a short successful run. The report is sited in main() after
    boot.parse(0) returns, the outermost boundary and the only place that knows the run is
    over, and it reads LEFTOVER INPUT rather than the refusal flag.

    ⚠ THE REFUSAL FLAG COULD NEVER HAVE NAMED THIS. Measured 2026-09-17, four shapes: a
    refusing define and an unterminated literal BOTH truncate, and `x = @@@` and a bad
    operator both run clean. Half the truncating cases raise no refusal at all, so the
    discriminator is MATCH FAILURE and the instrument has to read the mark.

    ⚠ THIS FILE TRUNCATES BY DESIGN AND HAS NO REACHABLE stop(). Safe under H5 -- a
    truncating run is not a HANGING run; it exits 0 promptly and cannot hold the suite.
    Its terminal marker is the ABANDONED line itself, emitted after the parse RETURNS, so
    a truncation cannot fake it.

    THE ROWS
      TR-1  the statement before the bad define runs -- the anti-vacuity sibling, without
            which TR-2's absence could just be a file that died at line one
      TR-2  the statement AFTER it does not -- pinned as a count of 0, read with TR-1
      TR-3  the abandonment is NAMED, with the file
      TR-4  ⚠ THE RESUME POINT, and this is the row that makes the report a DIAGNOSIS
            rather than an alarm: it quotes the first thing that was never parsed.
    docs/refusalScope.md carries the argument.  */
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking UnitTests Utilities list;
register(TruncT);
define
    trRoot;
    ;
cerr "TR-1 the statement before the bad define RAN":;
define
    trBad = trRoot.*trNope;
    ;
cerr "TR-2 the statement after it RAN -- it should not have":;
stop();
